  <section class="step" id="concept">
    <h2><span class="step-num">1</span> The Concept</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>DAX.OSI</strong> is a desktop application that simulates an entire operating system inside a single window — complete with a boot screen, login, desktop, and bundled apps.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>DOSI.CORE</strong> is the engine that powers it: window management, system settings, theming, animations, a UI control library, and a project/designer system for building new DOSI apps.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>Mental model:</strong> DAX.OSI is the <em>shell</em> you see and click. DOSI.CORE is the <em>library</em> it (and your future apps) depend on.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="step" id="run">
    <h2><span class="step-num">2</span> Run It</h2>
    <p>From the repository root, build and launch the shell:</p>
<pre><code>git clone https://github.com/Kruox/DAX.OSI-Master.git
cd DAX.OSI-Master
dotnet build
dotnet run --project DAX.OSI</code></pre>
    <p>On first launch a <code>SystemSettings.json</code> file is created next to the executable. You can edit it to change the default accent or fullscreen behavior.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" id="bootflow">
    <h2><span class="step-num">3</span> The Boot Flow</h2>
    <p>What happens when you press play:</p>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="flow-box">Program.Main<small>App startup</small></div>
      <div class="flow-box">App.Initialize<small>Theme + SystemCore</small></div>
      <div class="flow-box">BootScreen<small>Splash + animation</small></div>
      <div class="flow-box">LoginScreen<small>UserManager</small></div>
      <div class="flow-box">DesktopScreen<small>WindowManager</small></div>
    </div>
    <p>
      <code>ScreenManager</code> swaps between these full-screen surfaces, while <code>WindowManager</code> takes over once the desktop appears, hosting every opened app as a draggable, snappable <code>DOSIWindow</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" id="core">
    <h2><span class="step-num">4</span> Using DOSI.CORE</h2>
    <p>Most system features go through a small set of static entry points:</p>
<pre><code>// Boot the engine + load SystemSettings.json
SystemCore.Initialize();

// Apply the saved accent to all DOSI controls
AccentManager.Instance.InitializeFromSettings();

// Show a window on the desktop
WindowManager.Instance.Open(new MyAppWindow());

// Push a toast notification
DOSIPopNotification.Show("Welcome", "DAX.OSI is ready.");

// Cleanly shut the system down
SystemShutdown.Begin();</code></pre>
    <p>The <code>DOSI.CORE.UIComponents</code> namespace provides the styled controls: <code>DOSIWindow</code>, <code>DOSIButton</code>, <code>DOSITextBox</code>, <code>DOSIDialog</code>, <code>DOSICodeEditor</code>, and more — all themed by the active <code>AccentManager</code>.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="step" id="firstapp">
    <h2><span class="step-num">5</span> Your First DOSI App</h2>
    <p>A DOSI app is, at its simplest, a class that derives from <code>DOSIWindow</code> and is launched through <code>WindowManager</code>:</p>
<pre><code>using DOSI.CORE.UIComponents;
using DOSI.CORE.UIComponents.WindowManagement;

public class HelloApp : DOSIWindow
{
    public HelloApp()
    {
        Title  = "Hello, DOSI!";
        Width  = 480;
        Height = 280;

        var button = new DOSIButton { Content = "Click me" };
        button.Click += (_, _) =>
            DOSIPopNotification.Show("Hi", "You clicked the button!");

        Content = button;
    }
}

// Launch it from anywhere in the shell:
WindowManager.Instance.Open(new HelloApp());</code></pre>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>Tip:</strong> For richer apps, explore <code>DOSI.CORE.ProjectSystem</code> and <code>DOSI.CORE.Designer</code> — they let you author <code>DOSIProject</code> packages with a visual form designer and compile them into apps that register with <code>DOSIPublishedAppRegistry</code>.
    </div>
  </section>

